<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Agathyrsi</h1><p class="st-meta">550 BCE – 400 BCE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Gold-rich people of Transylvania described by Herodotus, known for tattoos, luxury and refusing to join the Scythians against Persia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> places the Agathyrsi along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Maris</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mures-river">Mureș</a>) in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-transylvania">Transylvania</a> in the 6th century BCE. Their origins are debated: they may have been a Scythian-influenced elite over a Thracian population, and Greek myth made their founder Agathyrsus a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> describes them as wearing much gold, tattooing themselves and holding wives in common so that all would be kin. Their king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spargapeithes">Spargapeithes</a> killed the Scythian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariapeithes">Ariapeithes</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> invaded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/scythia">Scythia</a> around 513 BCE, they refused Scythian refugees entry to their land. Scythian-style graves in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-transylvania">Transylvania</a> are often linked to them.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the 5th century BCE they vanish from the record; their lands later appear as part of the territory of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/getae">Getae</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dacians">Dacians</a>, into whom they were probably absorbed.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/agathyrsi.jpg" alt="Agathyrsi" width="320" height="203"><figcaption>Image: Antiquistik · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Agathyrsi_in_the_Balkans_-_closeup.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>550 BCE – 400 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Spargapeithes</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a></dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-475&amp;focus=agathyrsi" aria-label="Agathyrsi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5271 566 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Agathyrsi lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5591 822h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5591 822h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Romania. <a href="/atlas?year=-475&amp;focus=agathyrsi">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

